<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  com.twinvpn.twinvpnd — the authority's LaunchDaemon. Installed at
  /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.twinvpn.twinvpnd.plist.

  Authority: ADR-0016 §11.2 (the macOS component table and its "macOS,
  normatively" paragraph), §11.5 (the macOS durability row), §11.6 (the start
  sequence), §11.9 (the hardening table), PS-1, PS-9, PS-10, PS-11, PS-17,
  PS-18; ADR-0017 §11.2's macOS row and MI-A3.

  ================================================== WHAT THIS JOB IS, EXACTLY

  ADR-0016 §11.2's macOS row lists five components:

    com.twinvpn.sysext   NE system extension, root      <- THE AUTHORITY
    com.twinvpn.ksd      LaunchDaemon, root, minimal    <- the KS-19 artifact
    TwinVPN.app          per-user, sandboxed
    twinvpn              CLI, user
    twinvpn-unblock      privileged, separate executable

  and "macOS, normatively" says "the system extension is the authority".

  **This job is therefore NOT the authority in ADR-0016's sense**, and the
  divergence is deliberate and reported rather than papered over. It exists
  because the seam this product actually binds is the RUST crate, not
  twinvpn.h's F-9 vtable: ownership.md §8 W-24 and W-25 record that the vtable
  carries no socket capability, no interface enumeration, no installed_ruleset
  read-back and no current_generation, so a Swift system extension bound only to
  the C ABI cannot do NAT traversal and cannot produce a ProtectionAssertion at
  all. This daemon hosts the Rust core and the macOS platform adapter, owns pf
  and the MI endpoint, and the system extension is a thin packet-tunnel
  provider over the same core.

  THE CONSEQUENCE, STATED: there are two root components of ours besides ksd,
  and ADR-0016 §11.2's macOS row names only one. Reconciling that is the
  integration lead's, not this domain's. See shells/macos/README.md §7.

  ================================================== WHAT THIS PLIST CANNOT DO

  ADR-0016 §11.9's hardening table has a rich Linux column
  (ProtectSystem=strict, PrivateDevices, RestrictAddressFamilies,
  SystemCallFilter, MemoryDenyWriteExecute, …). launchd has NO equivalent for
  any of it. On macOS those controls come from three other places:

    - the hardened runtime and library validation, set at CODESIGN time
      (see packaging/SIGNING.md), which is the MemoryDenyWriteExecute analogue;
    - the App Sandbox, which a root LaunchDaemon does not use;
    - the kernel's own SIP, which is not ours to configure.

  So a reader must NOT infer from the shortness of this file that the macOS
  authority is less contained than the Linux one — the containment simply is not
  expressed here. PS-17 requires a directive that fails to apply to be named
  rather than silently skipped; the honest statement is that most of §11.9's
  table has no launchd expression at all, and shells/macos/README.md §7 records
  it as a gap.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.twinvpn.twinvpnd</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/Library/Application Support/TwinVPN/twinvpnd</string>
    </array>

    <!-- PS-1: the authority is a supervised process whose lifetime is
         independent of any UI process or login session (R-25). RunAtLoad plus a
         system-domain LaunchDaemon is what makes it independent of a console
         seat. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      ADR-0016 §11.5's macOS supervisor row: KeepAlive={SuccessfulExit:false},
      launchd's 10 s throttle.

      Restart on a crash; do NOT restart on a clean exit, because a clean exit is
      what an administered stop looks like and restarting it would make a
      deliberate stop unachievable.

      **KeepAlive is not crash-loop containment.** §11.5 again: "launchd has no
      burst limit, so the authority maintains its OWN durable restart counter
      (S-40) and latches quarantine itself." PS-9's bounded containment and
      PS-10's "restart MUST NOT disarm enforcement" are discharged in twinvpnd's
      own code, not by these two keys. If this plist looks like it contains a
      crash loop, it does not; it only spaces one out.
    -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>10</integer>

    <!--
      ADR-0016 §11.7's macOS row: the service account is `_twinvpn` and the
      operator group is `_twinvpn_op`. The installer creates both; the authority
      never does.

      **This job nevertheless runs as root, and that is a stated gap rather than
      an oversight.** The Linux unit drops to User=twinvpn with
      AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_ADMIN, so it never runs as root at all
      (§11.2: "the authority MUST NOT continue as root 'just this once'"). macOS
      has no capability model: programming routes, opening a utun via
      PF_SYSTEM/SYSPROTO_CONTROL and running pfctl all require uid 0 outright.
      There is no macOS spelling of "CAP_NET_ADMIN and nothing else".

      The honest posture is: root, with the surface reduced by the fact that the
      Rust binary links no interpreter, has no plugin path, and reads no
      configuration file. `_twinvpn` remains the owner of the state directory and
      the MI endpoint's group, so the DAC boundary between the authority and an
      unprivileged client is real even though the authority itself is uid 0.
      Recorded in shells/macos/README.md §7.
    -->
    <key>UserName</key>
    <string>root</string>
    <key>GroupName</key>
    <string>wheel</string>

    <!-- Interactive latency without a UI: the authority answers MI calls and
         must not be App-Napped while a client is waiting. `Adaptive` lets
         launchd promote it when it has active work and demote it when idle. -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Adaptive</string>

    <!--
      ADR-0017 MI-A3: the endpoint's directory "MUST be created by the OS init
      system with a privileged owner and no non-privileged write", and "the
      agent MUST verify the directory's ownership and mode before binding and
      MUST refuse to bind into a directory it does not own."

      launchd has NO RuntimeDirectory= equivalent. macOS does not create
      /var/run/twinvpn for us. So the directory is created by the INSTALLER
      (install.sh, 0755 root:wheel) rather than by the supervisor, and twinvpnd
      verifies it at start exactly as the Linux agent does. That is a weaker
      discharge of MI-A3 than the Linux one — the installer runs once, the
      supervisor runs every boot, and a /var/run wiped by the OS on reboot would
      leave the directory absent rather than wrong. twinvpnd therefore treats an
      ABSENT directory as a startup failure (MGMT.UNAVAILABLE), never as licence
      to create one itself with whatever ownership it happens to have.

      DELIBERATELY NO `Sockets` KEY. ADR-0017 MI-A3 and §10.3 prohibit socket
      activation: "the activation socket outlives the agent, so a client
      connects successfully then hangs instead of getting MGMT.UNAVAILABLE."
      launchd socket activation has exactly that shape, so it is not used.

      DELIBERATELY NO `MachServices` KEY IN THIS WAVE. ADR-0017 §11.2's macOS
      row wants NSXPCConnection to `com.twinvpn.agent.mgmt` with audit-token
      attestation as the PREFERRED binding, and AF_UNIX at
      /var/run/twinvpn/mgmt.sock for non-XPC clients such as the CLI. This wave
      implements the AF_UNIX half only. Declaring the Mach service here without
      a server for it would advertise a channel that hangs — the same failure
      MI-A3 rejects socket activation for. Recorded in README §7.
    -->

    <key>SoftResourceLimits</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NumberOfFiles</key>
        <integer>4096</integer>
    </dict>

    <!-- Logging goes to os_log via the process itself; these paths catch a
         pre-logging-init panic and nothing else. ADR-0015 §11.4: never a key, a
         session key, a tunnel payload, a pairing secret or a token — and a
         panic message is none of those. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/dev/null</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/twinvpn/twinvpnd.err.log</string>

    <!--
      ADR-0016 Q10: no inherited search path, preload variable or plugin
      directory that could supply executable code to a privileged process.
      PATH is pinned so `pfctl`, `route` and `scutil` resolve to the system
      binaries and to nothing else. DYLD_* variables are absent, and the
      hardened runtime's library validation (SIGNING.md) is what makes their
      absence enforced rather than merely intended.

      TWINVPN_MGMT_SOCKET and TWINVPN_LOG_* are NOT set here: every one has a
      default and the default is the production value, so setting them would
      only create a second place for the truth to live.
    -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
